{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help","id":12124,"title":"Gaul pantheon and other help","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-16T09:33:56-08:00","id":12124,"title":"Gaul pantheon and other help","content":"I need a bit of help, can any body help me get a hold of the forbidden library, the book with the gauls and stuff...and a translation, though I can get that myself, but one more step gone would be nice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/622-showzilla","name":"showzilla","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-16T14:06:45-08:00","id":12282,"title":"","content":"We were the original translators. If you show proof of purchase of the book we can give you the english translation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1051-jsr","name":"JSR","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T04:50:15-08:00","id":27122,"title":"","content":"Read the book &quot;The Discovery of Middle Earth&quot; by Robb. It will give you useful insights.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T07:08:55-08:00","id":27160,"title":"","content":"Read the Robb book only if you want some fictional inspiration, as it is completely pseudo-academic. I'm not going to put it into the same category as <i>Chariots of the Gods</i> but it is not far off. He starts with a theory, that ancient druids (which it is problematic to just throw in druids as the default priest/learned class throughout all the lands that are considered Celtic) were expert mapmakers long before the use of longitude/latitude and that all of their key moments in history (such as battles) took place purposefully along these lines. The problem is that he primarily distorts or outright fabricates his evidence to suit this theory (for example a lot of his map, including the Via Heraclea, is drawn in a completely straight line and, as in the Heraclea example, many miles away from where scholars believe the sites/roads to be) . Oddly enough he sort of hints at the end of the book that he seems to admit doing just that, being consumed by his original imaginative thesis.<br /> <br /> If you want some fictional inspiration (I could see it being thrown into either Mage books along with some Scion ideas) go for it but don't look to this book for real insight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1007-merinoc","name":"Merinoc","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T09:42:55-08:00","id":27265,"title":"","content":"So a book about a theory about the druids is now referred to as &quot;pseudo academic?&quot; I guess they all are. Since all we truly know is from propaganda, and what little we can ascertain from archaeological digs. And what I believe that's regarded as &quot;true&quot; today was that Celt society was indeed far more complex and sophisticated than ancient Rome wanted anyone to know.<br /> <br /> Although even Robb <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/books/graham-robbs-theory-on-celtic-migrations.html?_r=0\" target=\"_blank\">admitted</a> <i>\u201cI knew this would look like a book written by a lunatic.\u201d</i> He also said (from the link) that he spent months trying to disprove his own theory...and couldn't.<br /> <br /> Hmf...I recall reading &quot;<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Brief-History-Druids/dp/0786709871/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1386695795&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=peter+berresford+ellis\" target=\"_blank\">A Brief History of the Druids</a>.&quot; Which I find a decent read about this elite class. It is of course a &quot;theory&quot; and probably just as &quot;pseudo-academic&quot; but equally as interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T13:05:01-08:00","id":27444,"title":"","content":"There <i>are</i> academic standards in place, Shadowflame, that help determine if a theory is valid or not. Just because you can't <i>disprove</i> a theory doesn't mean you can offer supporting evidence to <i>prove</i> it either. Or even hold it to a reasonable standard. I haven't read the book in question, but you seem to raise these issues a lot whenever someone talks about Celts. Yes, it's absolutely true that modern historians, archaeologists and anthropologists are lacking in primary sources for that culture because it was preliterate. That doesn't mean it's a free-for-all on what is and is not true, or that everything written by the Romans is automatically dismissed just because they're Romans and they're biased.<br /> <br /> If you want to determine if a book and the theories inside meet academic standards and are accepted by a significant portion of the academic community, just go ask some people in that community. Professors and scholars are typically pretty excited when people email them asking about their specialty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T13:28:03-08:00","id":27469,"title":"","content":"I think you're making my point. Or did Robb not offer any supporting evidence in his book?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T13:52:38-08:00","id":27500,"title":"","content":"You miss the part where I said I haven't read the book? I'm talking about general academic standards that you seem to have an issue with. Maybe I'm reading what you're saying here and on the old forums wrong, but every time someone mentions things about the Celts you disagree with, it seems that you start attacking the whole idea of academic credibility and currently accepted theories of pre-Roman Celtic culture. It's fine to disagree with those, but it's not like people are just making stuff up willy-nilly and getting academic support for what they say. And if someone you agree with is <i>not</i> getting academic support, it isn't because of a conspiracy or because people Can't Handle The Truth.<br /> <br /> If I am totally misrepresenting you, I apologize. Maybe you're not the same person that was claiming Gauls never practiced human sacrifice, but your wording seems very similar to that person's arguments.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T14:20:35-08:00","id":27529,"title":"","content":"[QUOTESo a book about a theory about the druids is now referred to as &quot;pseudo academic?&quot; I guess they all are][/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> Its not the fact that it is about druids that makes it pseudo-academic, its the actual content of Robb's book. His theory is based entirely on historical revisionism. I am in complete agreement that we do not know much about druidic culture but Robb chooses to pretty much ignore what little there is and make links with it and non-druidic culture that we are more familiar with, for example one of his bits of evidence for the existence of this map and its sites is that Hannibal marched through this one specific path. Problem is that &quot;path&quot; has been debated by historians even those just decades after the actual event, giving about a 100 mile range it could have possibly taken place in. He does this frequently with other battles and the sites of cities, ignoring what archaeological evidence and historical knowledge we do have. <br /> <br /> There is of course legitimate scholarly work that has been done on Celtic culture, those who are not &quot;making stuff up willy-nilly&quot; as you say. Robb should not be counted among this group, however.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1007-merinoc","name":"Merinoc","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T04:59:26-08:00","id":27901,"title":"","content":"Robb's book makes sense and uses the archeological facts. Heck he uses Caesar's Gallic wars, pointing out the clear logical problems with Caesar's propoganda text. Besides, Robb only suggests that the Druids were good at navigation on land, a vastly simpler problem than sea navigation. The alternate theory requires the Gauls to name towns for bridges and wait several centuries for the Romans to come along and build them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T05:44:13-08:00","id":27918,"title":"","content":"I have to admit that I haven't read the book.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/books/bookreviews/10372050/The-Ancient-Paths-Discovering-the-Lost-Map-of-Celtic-Europe-review.html\" target=\"_blank\">~~The Ancient Paths: Discovering the Lost Map of Celtic Europe, review</a><br /> <br /> But I certainly will.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T09:18:39-08:00","id":28031,"title":"","content":"That book was reviewed recently by another gaelic polytheist here <a href=\"http://celticscholar.wordpress.com/2013/11/20/mapping-the-lost-world-of-the-celts/\" target=\"_blank\">http://celticscholar.wordpress.com/2...-of-the-celts/</a>; He focuses his book reviews on celtic books so there should be some for the gaulish pantheon even if he is a gaelic polytheist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1381-herkles","name":"herkles","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T10:07:25-08:00","id":28071,"title":"","content":"I'll admit to being biased here, but even the synopsis of the book makes it sound unreliable. Flowery language aside, even Caesar and Caesar's enemies never made the claim that he &quot;utterly annihilated&quot; the Gauls. One sentence isn't nearly enough to condemn the entire book, but it certainly isn't very reassuring.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T10:28:28-08:00","id":28085,"title":"","content":"<i>~~When Julius Caesar swept through, on a tide of warfare and genocide that would lead his countryman Pliny to accuse him of humani generis iniuria, \u201ccrimes against humanity...\u201d</i><br /> *shrugs*<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T10:43:04-08:00","id":28092,"title":"","content":"No one is going to dispute that Caesar's legions killed a lot of Gauls and then proceeded to enslave even more of them, but the claim made is one of utter destruction. This is a ridiculous claim, because some of the Gallic peoples sided with the Romans, while even some of those that fought against Caesar managed to survive in significant numbers. For example, the Aedui were one of the foremost Gallic peoples to rise against Caesar, but still managed to last long enough to rebel against Tiberius.<br /> <br /> I haven't read the book, so I can't comment on its contents. However, the author's choice of language in this particular incidence suggests that his pen is flowing more freely than truth permits, which is rarely a good sign in academic works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T11:34:07-08:00","id":28141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Creticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28092#post28092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No one is going to dispute that Caesar's legions killed a lot of Gauls and then proceeded to enslave even more of them, but the claim made is one of utter destruction.</div> </div> </div> </div>It depends on how you're viewing &quot;utter destruction.&quot; If you mean in terms of personage of the race, then obviously not. Gauls survived well into the modern era. If you mean culturally, then there are strong arguments to support that view being correct. You could posit that part of the reason the northern (and island) pagan traditions survived more or less intact even after the arrival of Christianity is because they weren't forced into Roman cultural mores by conquering armies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T14:29:36-08:00","id":28305,"title":"","content":"if they hadn't been obliterated...I would think the druids would like to chime in here,","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T14:37:38-08:00","id":28308,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28141#post28141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It depends on how you're viewing &quot;utter destruction.&quot; If you mean in terms of personage of the race, then obviously not. Gauls survived well into the modern era. If you mean culturally, then there are strong arguments to support that view being correct. You could posit that part of the reason the northern (and island) pagan traditions survived more or less intact even after the arrival of Christianity is because they weren't forced into Roman cultural mores by conquering armies.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the complete sentence from the synopsis as seen in the review:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Within eight years the Celts of what is now France were utterly annihilated, and in another hundred years the Romans had overrun Britain. </div> </div> </div>It's not referring to the culture of the Gauls, but the Gauls themselves. Furthermore, Gallic culture became Romanized, which is not the same as a complete replacement with Roman culture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T15:27:18-08:00","id":28338,"title":"","content":"I refuse to believe that plenty of Gaulish culture survived without the druids. Since I firmly <i>believe</i> that they were the heart and soul of much of Gaul. The unfortunate reason why they had to be destroyed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T18:43:59-08:00","id":28442,"title":"","content":"See, you can totally believe that. It just hasn't got academic standing without sources and peer review.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T19:38:17-08:00","id":28475,"title":"","content":"I think my belief is safe, since that goes for anyone that claims anything certain about the druids.<br /> <br /> Peer reviewed paper on Gaul society without druids? Good luck finding that. That's only suitable...say for a game about Gaul's deities perhaps. (although heaven forbid anyone mention &quot;accuracy.&quot;)<br /> <br /> But if anyone finds anything different, by all means rewrite that wikipedia entry.<br /> <br /> The druids have always been a sore point of contention for me, since there's absolutely nothing certain about them. And I've looked all over. And even archaeology supports that uncertainty. All we have are propaganda pieces from a ruthless tyrant who needed the money he'd acquire from conquering other parts of the continent. Other ancient sources are just as frustrating. And the gods only knows they may all be correct.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T20:45:49-08:00","id":28507,"title":"","content":"Claiming that Gallic culture cannot exist without the druids sounds similar to claiming that Chinese culture cannot exist without the scholar-bureaucrats. It's a serious blow, but it's not going to stop the common people from passing their beliefs, stories, and values on to their children. Much of their culture changed because of Roman rule, but at the same time, much of their culture managed to last because of basic human nature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T05:39:44-08:00","id":28653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Creticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28507#post28507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Claiming that Gallic culture cannot exist without the druids sounds similar to claiming that Chinese culture cannot exist without the scholar-bureaucrats. It's a serious blow, but it's not going to stop the common people from passing their beliefs, stories, and values on to their children. Much of their culture changed because of Roman rule, but at the same time, much of their culture managed to last because of basic human nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'd compare Chinese cultures, and all the literary that goes with it, to Gaul and the druids?<br /> Wow, that's interesting.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T06:51:25-08:00","id":28688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28653#post28653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You'd compare Chinese cultures, and all the literary that goes with it, to Gaul and the druids?<br /> Wow, that's interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's on-the-spot rhetoric. Don't read too much into it.<br /> <br /> That said, even if the entire class of scholar-officials were suppressed and their writings burned, Chinese culture would have still survived. Changed, but still recognizable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T09:17:43-08:00","id":28763,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Creticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28688#post28688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, even if...</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah...&quot;if..&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T15:35:07-08:00","id":28966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Creticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28688#post28688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's on-the-spot rhetoric. Don't read too much into it.<br /> <br /> That said, even if the entire class of scholar-officials were suppressed and their writings burned, Chinese culture would have still survived. Changed, but still recognizable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Side note: what you described actually happened in the Ming dynasty. It happened again in more recent memory too, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T15:58:03-08:00","id":28973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28966#post28966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Side note: what you described actually happened in the Ming dynasty. It happened again in more recent memory too, of course.</div> </div> </div> </div>The incident I had in mind was under the Qin, which oversaw the end of the Hundred Schools of Thought. Came complete with the burning of books, the burying of scholars, and a second burning of books when the Chu-Han contention rolled into Xianyang before it was even a thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T17:21:55-08:00","id":29017,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Creticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=28973#post28973\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The incident I had in mind was under the Qin, which oversaw the end of the Hundred Schools of Thought. Came complete with the burning of books, the burying of scholars, and a second burning of books when the Chu-Han contention rolled into Xianyang before it was even a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>If I'm recalling correctly there were four major purges of the bureaucracy during the Ming dynasty and twice again under the Qing. I'm not too familiar with Qin or Han dynasty China, so I'll take your word for it. Worth pointing out that it was so common an occurrence that it actually takes place within the Celestial Bureaucracy in a few stories. So...yeah.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-12T18:19:12-08:00","id":29055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=29017#post29017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I'm recalling correctly there were four major purges of the bureaucracy during the Ming dynasty and twice again under the Qing. I'm not too familiar with Qin or Han dynasty China, so I'll take your word for it. Worth pointing out that it was so common an occurrence that it actually takes place within the Celestial Bureaucracy in a few stories. So...yeah.</div> </div> </div> </div>Are you referring to political purges? Because those happened on multiple occasions across multiple dynasties.<br /> <br /> For example, since I'm most familiar with the Eastern Han, the one that comes to mind for me is the series of proscriptions that came about because of fighting between the emperors and the families of their consorts, which resulted in the loss of confidence that brought about the 3 Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> The Qin purge was a bit more serious than that.<br /> <br /> Our information comes from biased sources, but it seems that Qin Shihuang sought to unite opinion by suppressing the schools of thought that had flourished before his unification. He burned the classics, he burned the histories, and he burned the political treatises. People who discussed the banned material were executed, as were people who used historical examples to criticize his rule. In fact, Qin Shihuang is infamous for having hundreds of scholars buried alive in the capital, while thousands were sent north as penal labor to build the Great Wall. The Qin did collect copies of the banned books in their records, but those went up in flames with the rest of Xianyang when Xiang Yu rolled through in 206 BC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-13T08:04:01-08:00","id":29310,"title":"","content":"What Robb makes a case for is that the Celts were more advanced than we had thought and he proves this from the avialible evidence. Why is that iffy?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-13T13:29:06-08:00","id":29495,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/12124-gaul-pantheon-and-other-help?p=29310#post29310\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What Robb makes a case for is that the Celts were more advanced than we had thought and he proves this from the avialible evidence. Why is that iffy?</div> </div> </div> </div>He's making extraordinary claims without the extraordinary evidence needed to back them up.<br /> <br /> Read this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-the-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-1.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/uploads/3/7/5/9/3759274/281674_orig.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-the-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-1.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Review of Graham Robb's \"The Discovery of Middle Earth\" (Part 1) </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-the-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-1.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-the-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-1.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Thanks to the generosity of a regular reader of this blog, I&rsquo;ve received a copy of Graham Robb&rsquo;s new book The Discovery of Middle Earth: Mapping the Lost World of the Celts . (Published as The... </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-2.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/uploads/3/7/5/9/3759274/1417180_orig.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-2.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Review of Graham Robb's \"Discovery of Middle Earth\" (Part 2) </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-2.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-2.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> I&rsquo;ve completed reading Part One of Graham Robb&rsquo;s The Discovery of Middle Earth , and it goes a ways toward rehabilitating some of the claims presented less convincingly in the preface and Chapter... </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-3.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/uploads/3/7/5/9/3759274/blog-square_orig.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-3.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Review of Graham Robb's \"Discovery of Middle Earth\" (Part 3) </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-3.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/review-of-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-part-3.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> In this edition, we move forward to Part Two of Graham Robb&rsquo;s The Discovery of Middle Earth , in which the author attempts to make the case for advanced Druid science, and in so doing he comes to... </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/reviewing-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-final-part.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/uploads/3/7/5/9/3759274/blog-square_orig.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/reviewing-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-final-part.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Reviewing Graham Robb's \"Discovery of Middle Earth\" (Final Part) </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/reviewing-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-final-part.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.jasoncolavito.com/1/post/2013/11/reviewing-graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth-final-part.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> After a bit of a break to give me time to read ahead, we move forward to Part Three of Graham Robb&rsquo;s The Discovery of Middle Earth , in which the author attempts to defend the premises elaborated... </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> As for the Celts, speaking about them as a whole is problematic because the Celts stretched from Ireland to Central Europe, never mind the Celtic peoples that settled in Egypt and the Near East. Some of the Celtic peoples were pretty advanced, while others were not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-13T16:35:52-08:00","id":29602,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What Robb makes a case for is that the Celts were more advanced than we had thought and he proves this from the avialible evidence. Why is that iffy?</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/oct/27/ancient-paths-graham-robb-review\" target=\"_blank\">Review-one</a><br /> <a href=\"http://www.salon.com/2013/11/03/the_celts_were_smarter_than_we_think/\" target=\"_blank\">Review-two</a><br /> <a href=\"http://atriptoireland.com/2013/11/08/rediscovering-the-lost-world-of-the-celts/\" target=\"_blank\">Review-three</a><br /> <a href=\"http://literaryreview.co.uk/hoare_10_13.php\" target=\"_blank\">Review-four</a><br /> <br /> I see it as less iffy, and more of an encouragement for debate and study.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","user":"Shadowflame","date":". 12-13-2013, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-16T12:47:23-08:00","id":31135,"title":"","content":"Some of the best evidence is clearly proved already. Take the rejection of the commplace assumption that the Gauls were technologically primitive. He shows that the chairiots buried with the Celts were not of a type that anyone would take off road. The wheels were of a type that requiered roads. This strongly suggests the Celts had good roads. Robb further shows, from conventional sources, that the vast majority of the terms used for animal powered vehicles used in Latin came from Gaulish terms and words. Scholars have known for generations that the majority of Latin terms, for what would have been a major area of technology, came from Gaulish. Caesar makes better time on Gaulish roads than he would on Roman ones, this is proven from Caesar's own texts.<br /> <br /> Over and over, Robb uses physical evidence and primary sources to back is claims. The claims may be incredible, but the evidence was in plain sight.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}